WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:01.810
Who is Cad Bane?

00:00:01.810 --> 00:00:04.460
How does his origin tie to the original Star
Wars trilogy?

00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:06.710
And is he really just a Boba Fett clone?

00:00:06.710 --> 00:00:10.780
Keep watching for the untold truth of the
galaxy's most terrifying bounty hunter.

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:16.090
Today, it's hard to imagine the era of Clone
Wars-dominated Star Wars media without thinking

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:20.789
of Cad Bane, arguably one of the most famous
characters created for that original series.

00:00:20.789 --> 00:00:24.220
Initially, though, Cad Bane wasn't supposed
to be a part of the show at all.

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:28.810
In fact, the original landscape for The Clone
Wars had no room for third-party players like

00:00:28.810 --> 00:00:29.810
bounty hunters.

00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:34.260
Instead, the focus of the show's stories would
exclusively remain on Republic and Separatist

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:35.260
forces.

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:38.580
As production on the show progressed, however,
these plans changed.

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:42.930
Bounty hunters gradually began to be incorporated
into the show's story, though even by that

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:45.630
point Cad Bane's introduction was no sure
thing.

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:49.870
On the contrary, his role in The Clone Wars
was to be filled by pre-existing characters

00:00:49.870 --> 00:00:55.200
from Star Wars lore, such as Durge.

00:00:55.200 --> 00:01:02.370
However, once George Lucas suggested taking
the character in a visual approach more inspired

00:01:02.370 --> 00:01:05.239
by Westerns, a more original idea emerged.

00:01:05.239 --> 00:01:08.960
From the ashes of these inaugural concepts
came Cad Bane.

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:12.890
The distinctive look of the aliens in the
Star Wars galaxy is one of the best parts

00:01:12.890 --> 00:01:13.979
of this franchise.

00:01:13.979 --> 00:01:19.070
Honing down the finer intricacies of the appearances
of these creatures is a difficult task, however,

00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:22.350
and inspiration for an alien design can strike
from anywhere.

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:26.450
In the case of Cad Bane, the Clone Wars crew
found his look in the distant past.

00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:30.700
Specifically, his design came from a piece
of unused concept art for an alien character

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:35.710
in the original Star Wars trilogy; one with
guns in holsters and a gigantic-rimmed hat.

00:01:35.710 --> 00:01:39.929
The artist could never have imagined it decades
earlier, but this figure was exactly what

00:01:39.929 --> 00:01:43.139
the Clone Wars team was looking for when it
came to Cad Bane.

00:01:43.139 --> 00:01:47.209
Clone Wars producer Dave Filoni has since
explained that, although this may sound like

00:01:47.209 --> 00:01:51.140
an unlikely scenario, it's a common practice
for Star Wars media.

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:55.159
George Lucas and other artists would often
come up with character names or alien designs

00:01:55.159 --> 00:02:00.079
in the 1970s and promptly abandon them, only
for them to find the perfect home for them

00:02:00.079 --> 00:02:01.479
decades down the road.

00:02:01.479 --> 00:02:05.119
When you're a franchise like Star Wars that
spans for years and years, you never know

00:02:05.119 --> 00:02:08.179
when an idea might get a second lease on life.

00:02:08.179 --> 00:02:12.150
Cad Bane engages in a lot of behavior that's
pretty harsh by the standards of villains

00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:13.700
on animated kids TV shows.

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:18.129
In his first appearance on The Clone Wars,
he kills a Galactic Senator by shooting them

00:02:18.129 --> 00:02:22.450
in the back, while a subsequent appearance
depicts him torturing a Jedi for important

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:23.450
information.

00:02:23.450 --> 00:02:27.439
Whenever Bane shows up, he's bound to engage
in ruthless violence — which is exactly

00:02:27.439 --> 00:02:32.460
why this particular bounty hunter has secured
such a formidable reputation among fans of

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:33.460
the franchise.

00:02:33.460 --> 00:02:36.420
However, one place Bane couldn't cross the
line was smoking.

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:40.410
On one of the bonus features on the box set
for the fourth season of The Clone Wars, Dave

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:45.260
Filoni comments that Bane's trademark toothpick
was originally supposed to be a cigarette.

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:48.840
This would've aligned with the Western and
noir inspirations for the character, as these

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:53.269
two genres are known for feature characters
who aren't afraid to light one up every now

00:02:53.269 --> 00:02:54.269
and then.

00:02:54.269 --> 00:02:58.920
The difference was, of course, that Bane featured
on an animated kid's show, rather than, say,

00:02:58.920 --> 00:02:59.920
Casablanca.

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:02.360
"Here's looking at you, kid."

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:06.890
So the cigarettes were ditched, and Bane was
forced to satisfy his penchant for unruly

00:03:06.890 --> 00:03:09.769
behavior in other, more vicious ways.

00:03:09.769 --> 00:03:14.290
Star Wars: The Clone Wars ground to a halt
in the spring of 2013, just a few months after

00:03:14.290 --> 00:03:19.160
Disney purchased Lucasfilm — a sudden development
that left multiple seasons' worth of stories

00:03:19.160 --> 00:03:20.489
on the back-burner.

00:03:20.489 --> 00:03:25.599
Even after two bursts of Clone Wars episodes
were released across 2014 and 2020, much of

00:03:25.599 --> 00:03:27.860
the show's content remained unreleased.

00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:31.970
This is the fate that befell a proposed storyline
that would have seen Cad Bane returning, this

00:03:31.970 --> 00:03:34.380
time as a mentor to a young Boba Fett.

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:38.790
Though Bane was a central figure in these
episodes, this arc was primarily a Boba Fett

00:03:38.790 --> 00:03:42.819
origin story, right down to him getting his
iconic Mandalorian armor.

00:03:42.819 --> 00:03:46.709
The latter development was supposed to occur
during a shoot-out between Bane and Fett,

00:03:46.709 --> 00:03:51.310
which would have capped off the duo's journey
from student and master to straight-up enemies.

00:03:51.310 --> 00:03:55.310
Made for the Season 5 development cycle of
The Clone Wars, it's unknown why this storyline

00:03:55.310 --> 00:03:58.799
wasn't finished and released for either of
the show's brief revivals.

00:03:58.799 --> 00:04:03.239
However, one reason could be that the Fett
v. Bane showdown ended with Bane's death.

00:04:03.239 --> 00:04:07.629
It's possible that the creative team behind
The Clone Wars opted to scrap this episode

00:04:07.629 --> 00:04:11.290
so that Bane could remain alive and well for
future appearances.

00:04:11.290 --> 00:04:16.269
It's hard not to see some similarities between
Cad Bane and Boba Fett, and not just because

00:04:16.269 --> 00:04:17.950
they're both bounty hunters.

00:04:17.950 --> 00:04:22.259
Both are men of few words, but aren't afraid
to show force when the moment calls for it.

00:04:22.259 --> 00:04:27.030
They also sport some of the most distinctive
outfits in the Star Wars galaxy, and are particularly

00:04:27.030 --> 00:04:29.000
well-known for their snazzy headgear.

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:33.130
However, the team behind The Clone Wars was
particularly conscious of Bane coming off

00:04:33.130 --> 00:04:37.660
as just a clone of a more popular bounty hunter,
and did everything they could to make him

00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:38.660
unique.

00:04:38.660 --> 00:04:42.741
Talking to StarWars.com directly after Bane's
first appearance on The Clone Wars, director

00:04:42.741 --> 00:04:48.100
Giancarlo Volpe made sure to emphasize that,
unlike Fett, Bane "doesn't take prisoners."

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:52.190
Though a small distinction, this detail already
suggests a world of difference between the

00:04:52.190 --> 00:04:53.710
two bounty hunters.

00:04:53.710 --> 00:04:57.910
Bane is supposed to be an even darker version
of Fett, and he's an important entity to have

00:04:57.910 --> 00:05:02.380
lingering around as an adolescent Fett navigates
the hate he has in his heart over the death

00:05:02.380 --> 00:05:03.380
of his father.

00:05:03.380 --> 00:05:08.639
Notably, Bane's ruthlessness and capacity
for evil serves to demonstrate what Fett might

00:05:08.639 --> 00:05:10.510
become, if he doesn't change his ways.

00:05:10.510 --> 00:05:14.750
Quietly introducing this thematic detail is
one of the many reasons that Bane comes off

00:05:14.750 --> 00:05:17.680
as more than just a Boba Fett doppelganger.

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:21.790
Cad Bane, like so many other important aspects
of the Star Wars mythos, is heavily-inspired

00:05:21.790 --> 00:05:23.090
by classic cinema.

00:05:23.090 --> 00:05:27.539
In particular, everything about the character's
fashion sense owes much to Western protagonists.

00:05:27.539 --> 00:05:32.270
Elsewhere, one of the character's earliest
appearances introduces Bane draped in darkness

00:05:32.270 --> 00:05:35.740
and shards of light, like a character from
a noir movie.

00:05:35.740 --> 00:05:40.319
Though existing in a sci-fi world populated
by futuristic technology, Cad Bane is very

00:05:40.319 --> 00:05:42.890
much evocative of vintage pop culture.

00:05:42.890 --> 00:05:46.860
So it shouldn't come as much of a surprise
to hear that voice actor Corey Burton looked

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:49.690
to the past for inspiration on how to voice
Cad Bane.

00:05:49.690 --> 00:05:54.099
Speaking to StarWars.com, Burton revealed
that the primary inspiration for the character's

00:05:54.099 --> 00:05:56.880
vocals was the distinctive voice of Peter
Lorre.

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:01.250
Specifically, Burton took particular cues
from Lorre's more subdued work on assorted

00:06:01.250 --> 00:06:03.229
Lux Radio Theater programs.

00:06:03.229 --> 00:06:07.910
"Observe what follows: in a few seconds, unconsciousness.

00:06:07.910 --> 00:06:11.669
In two minutes, or three minutes, death."

00:06:11.669 --> 00:06:16.039
However, Burton made sure to emphasize that
Bane is not just a redux of Lorre's voice

00:06:16.039 --> 00:06:20.460
— he pointed out that both he and the Clone
Wars crew would use this iconic classic movie

00:06:20.460 --> 00:06:25.960
actor as a starting basis for the performance,
before taking it in new directions.

00:06:25.960 --> 00:06:29.300
Given how much talent from The Clone Wars
was ported over to Star Wars: Rebels, you'd

00:06:29.300 --> 00:06:33.690
assume that the former show's recurring antagonist,
Cad Bane, should have been right in line for

00:06:33.690 --> 00:06:34.830
a guest appearance.

00:06:34.830 --> 00:06:38.860
The likelihood of that happening seemed to
increase once Clone Wars fan-favorite characters

00:06:38.860 --> 00:06:44.270
such as Ahsoka Tano and Captain Rex made appearances
on the program, and the series even continued

00:06:44.270 --> 00:06:46.449
Darth Maul's storyline from The Clone Wars.

00:06:46.449 --> 00:06:50.150
If these familiar faces could show up, who's
to say Cad Bane couldn't?

00:06:50.150 --> 00:06:54.180
Unfortunately for Cad Bane fans, the bounty
hunter was missing from the entire run of

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:55.180
Rebels.

00:06:55.180 --> 00:06:59.099
Even when other Clone Wars scoundrels showed
up, Bane was simply nowhere to be found.

00:06:59.099 --> 00:07:03.520
However, eagle-eyed viewers may have spotted
the closest thing to a Bane cameo on the show

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:08.229
in one particular Rebels episode, in the form
of Bane graffiti on a wall behind Sabine.

00:07:08.229 --> 00:07:12.330
Though just a background detail, this Easter
egg reminded viewers that Bane is very much

00:07:12.330 --> 00:07:15.550
alive and well in this era of Star Wars.

00:07:15.550 --> 00:07:19.949
Even before Disney bought Lucasfilm, Star
Wars TV shows were all about bringing in familiar

00:07:19.949 --> 00:07:22.250
faces for surprise appearances.

00:07:22.250 --> 00:07:26.050
Just look at how Jabba the Hutt factored into
the Clone Wars movie or the guest appearance

00:07:26.050 --> 00:07:29.050
by Greedo in a Season 3 episode of the series.

00:07:29.050 --> 00:07:33.580
This practice has only become more pronounced
in recent years as Lucasfilm attempts to reinforce

00:07:33.580 --> 00:07:37.889
a sense of unified continuity across all its
various Star Wars properties.

00:07:37.889 --> 00:07:42.919
One example of this was Cad Bane making a
surprise appearance in the 2021 series Star

00:07:42.919 --> 00:07:44.720
Wars: The Bad Batch.

00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:48.830
Producer Jennifer Corbett explained to Skywalking
Through the Neverland that having Bane appear

00:07:48.830 --> 00:07:52.970
on The Bad Batch was more than just providing
a dose of fan service to viewers.

00:07:52.970 --> 00:07:57.280
The show had already featured the leads facing
off against bounty hunter Fennec Shand, and,

00:07:57.280 --> 00:08:01.360
following that formidable opponent, the writers
wanted to introduce an even greater threat

00:08:01.360 --> 00:08:03.080
for the protagonists to battle.

00:08:03.080 --> 00:08:07.270
This is where Cad Bane came into play, as
Corbett and the other Bad Batch personnel

00:08:07.270 --> 00:08:11.580
felt he was one of the most ruthless figures
these characters could go toe-to-toe with.

00:08:11.580 --> 00:08:15.789
And if bringing back this adversary reinforced
the grander fictional universe The Bad Batch

00:08:15.789 --> 00:08:18.889
occupied, well, that was just gravy, too.

00:08:18.889 --> 00:08:22.580
When Cad Bane reappears in The Bad Batch,
making his first appearance in a canonical

00:08:22.580 --> 00:08:26.449
Star Wars property in almost a decade, he
looks a little different than viewers might

00:08:26.449 --> 00:08:27.449
remember.

00:08:27.449 --> 00:08:31.100
Bane hasn't undergone a radical transformation,
but he's got a new ship, and his clothes have

00:08:31.100 --> 00:08:32.510
been tweaked a little, too.

00:08:32.510 --> 00:08:36.130
These weren't incidental alterations, though
— they were intentional enhancements to

00:08:36.130 --> 00:08:41.070
the character meant to take him into new directions,
while also paying tribute to unrealized storylines

00:08:41.070 --> 00:08:43.280
involving Bane from The Clone Wars.

00:08:43.280 --> 00:08:46.050
Director Brad Rau explained to Skywalking
Through Neverland:

00:08:46.050 --> 00:08:50.320
"For the design of Cad Bane, we were looking
at some designs that had never gone into production

00:08:50.320 --> 00:08:53.970
that were in progress in Clone Wars [...] It
was a lot of fun to sort of take them out

00:08:53.970 --> 00:08:58.120
of the garage and tinker with it and push
it and rework it a little bit so that Cad

00:08:58.120 --> 00:09:02.160
Bane and his ride felt familiar and yet a
little bit more nuanced and more detailed."

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:06.550
These differences also helped to reinforce
the passage of time between Bane's last appearance

00:09:06.550 --> 00:09:10.060
and the era of Star Wars history in which
The Bad Batch occurs.

00:09:10.060 --> 00:09:14.480
By taking this visual route, the show could
tip its hat towards past Star Wars television

00:09:14.480 --> 00:09:18.560
while also offering up something new for a
fan-favorite character.

00:09:18.560 --> 00:09:22.450
Bringing Cad Bane back for The Bad Batch didn't
just offer an opportunity to make fans of

00:09:22.450 --> 00:09:24.550
the bounty hunter as pleased as punch.

00:09:24.550 --> 00:09:28.459
It was also a chance to bring to life certain
story beats that The Clone Wars never got

00:09:28.459 --> 00:09:30.320
to play out before its cancellation.

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:33.900
Of course, given how much time had passed
between the first end of the The Clone Wars'

00:09:33.900 --> 00:09:39.060
in 2013 and the 2021 debut of The Bad Batch,
it was clear that the original concepts for

00:09:39.060 --> 00:09:41.800
Bane storylines couldn't just be reused whole
cloth.

00:09:41.800 --> 00:09:46.670
But they could be reinterpreted to fit newly
introduced characters in the Star Wars mythos.

00:09:46.670 --> 00:09:50.480
In an interview with ScreenRant, Brad Rau
revealed that Cad Bane's appearance in The

00:09:50.480 --> 00:09:55.160
Bad Batch was steeped deeply in unused designs
and storylines from The Clone Wars.

00:09:55.160 --> 00:09:59.459
The big showdown between Bane and new character
Hunter was even based on the animation and

00:09:59.459 --> 00:10:03.910
direction of the unused battle between Bane
and Boba Fett from the earlier series.

00:10:03.910 --> 00:10:04.910
Rau explained:

00:10:04.910 --> 00:10:08.959
"So it's more of us as fans, behind the scenes,
wanting to pay homage to that."

00:10:08.959 --> 00:10:14.000
When it comes to this galaxy far, far away,
no idea is ever really gone.

